   "subject": "CFA with Likert-type indicators",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">I need to perform a CFA where all indicators are 5-point Likert-type\r\nitems. I want to report the outputs of:</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">1. parameterEstimates(mod, standardized = TRUE)</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">2. fitMeasures(fit)</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">3. reliability(fit)</font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Since thresholds are not symmetric, there is a significant difference in all outputs if I\r\nconsider the analysis in two different datasets, one of them having indicators\r\nof type &quot;numeric&quot;, and another having indicators of type\r\n&quot;ordered&quot;. I would like you to confirm that the correct analysis must\r\nconsider indicators of type &quot;ordered&quot;. Moreover, as I understand, lavaan\r\nautomatically selects the suitable estimator and other stuff; nevertheless, do\r\nyou recommend to write additional options in the functions 1. - 3.?</font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><br></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Monaco\"><br></span></p>\r\n\r\n</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Hp45suhRAQAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2017-08-06T16:23:11Z",
   "subject": "Re: CFA with Likert-type indicators",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">I would like you to confirm that the correct analysis must\r\nconsider indicators of type &quot;ordered&quot;. </font></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>They are ordered, so the &quot;correct&quot; analysis is the one that takes that fact into account.  There is some evidence that results are nearly unbiased when treating 5-category ordered indicators as continuous (<a href=\"http://psych.colorado.edu/~willcutt/pdfs/Rhemtulla_2012.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://psych.colorado.edu/~willcutt/pdfs/Rhemtulla_2012.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426934201000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0pzC8A_GlSzjrDDB2aJcpr\">http://psych.colorado.edu/~willcutt/pdfs/Rhemtulla_2012.pdf</a>), but you need 7 categories if the thresholds are asymmetric.  So you are probably better off treating them as ordered.<div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Moreover, as I understand, lavaan\r\nautomatically selects the suitable estimator and other stuff; nevertheless, do\r\nyou recommend to write additional options in the functions 1. - 3.?</font></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>If your variables are of class &quot;<font face=\"courier new, monospace\">ordered</font>&quot; in the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">data.frame</font>, or if you tell lavaan they are ordered, lavaan will appropriately use DWLS estimation with robust <i>SE</i>s and test statistic.</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/cat.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/cat.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426934201000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0-KMNBCS9Go2g_C6I2Gt_6\">http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/cat.html</a><br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426934201000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0rp728wniZClpylnCfMQEd\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
